  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM EST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nThe upper low that brought the frigid and windy conditions is \ncontinuing its journey northeast through the New England Region \ntonight. We're seeing rainfall mostly tapered off as the best \nlift with this system also moves out with it, but can't completely\nrule out a very brief drizzle or flurry around midnight for a few\npeople in southwest Virginia.\n\nClouds will try and clear out overnight as we sit in a lull between \nsystems. Have continued with the slower overnight clearing compared \nto most models as they typically don't hold onto clouds in \nwest/northwest flow as long as they end up lasting. This doesn't \nmean some people won't see some stars tonight with breaks expected \ncloser to sunrise...And areas that do see breaks in the clouds will \nhave the best chance at seeing fog develop in the morning from the \nrecent rainfall.\n\nTemperatures are expected to rebound by a noticeable amount \nSunday with sunnier skies and ridging building in. The sun \ncombined with lighter (but not calm) winds means it should be much\nmore pleasant outside Sunday versus the previous several days. \nSunday evening the strengthening upper trough will move into the \nregion from the west ushering in our next weather-maker. \nWidespread showers incoming for Sunday exiting Sunday night. \nEnsembles and deterministic models show some elevated instability \ngenerally south of interstate 40 so isolated embedded thunder is \nexpected. With the strength of the jet associated with this system\n(50+ knots at 850 mb) a stronger storm in southeast TN could \npossible bring down some of those low level jet winds towards the \nsurface. So while the chances look very low, cannot completely \nrule out an isolated wind gust of 50+ mph with a strong storm in \nsoutheast TN on Sunday. Ensemble QPF along with WPC depicts QPF \namounts from 0.5 to 0.75 inch of most locations for this event.\n\nThe strengthening jet with this system will also likely induce a \nmountain wave event across the southern Appalachians on Sunday. A \nHigh Wind Watch is in effect with models currently depicting \nwinds in excess of 60 mph in the most mountain wave prone areas. \nElsewhere, tightening pressure gradient associated with frontal \nboundary will likely produce breezy conditions for Sunday \nafternoon and evening.\n\nFor Monday through Thursday, ensemble cluster analysis shows surface \nand upper ridging will produce mainly dry conditions and a warming \ntrend. Highs will climb back into the 60s with temperatures around \n10 degrees above normal by mid to late week.",
